doc.para:
Exception Object
　Various information about the exception is thrown along with an object of a class called Exception. This object is called an exception object. You can receive this in catch.
　For example, you can write a script like the following:

	try
	{
		loadImages("nothing.jpeg"); // Processing that might cause an exception
	}
	catch (e) // Receive the exception in the variable e
	{
		// e.message represents the message string thrown with the exception.
		// This may explain the reason why the exception occurred.
		inform("Failed to load image.\n"+e.message);
	}

　In this way, by writing the receiving variable inside parentheses after catch, you can receive the exception object in that variable.
　The scope of this variable begins inside the block following catch and ends together with this block. This variable does not need to be declared in advance.

throw Statement
　You can also throw exceptions. To throw an exception, use the throw keyword.
　For example, you can write a script like the following:

	function pow2(n)
	{
		// Calculates 2 to the power of n; n must be a positive integer
		if(n<0) throw new Exception("Negative numbers cannot be specified.");
			// ↑ Causes an exception
		return 1<<n;
	}

　If a negative number such as -1 is passed as an argument to this function, an exception will occur. The statement that throws the exception is as follows:


throw new Exception("Negative numbers cannot be specified.");


　Regarding new Exception("Negative numbers cannot be specified."), this creates an object of the Exception class with a string as an argument. For details, please refer to the Exception class. Then, this newly created object is thrown using the throw keyword.
　What can be thrown with the throw keyword is not limited to objects of the Exception class. You can throw numbers, strings, or references to functions. However, throwing objects of the Exception class, or classes derived from it, is recommended. This is because when caught, you only need to write code assuming that an object of the Exception class will come in the catch block.

Rethrowing an Exception
　Even though an exception was caught in catch, a try that might still receive the exception may be declared somewhere else; in such cases, you can rethrow the exception.
　You can write a script like the following:

	function tryloadimage()
	{
		try
		{
			primaryLayer.loadImages("test1.bmp"); // Try reading test1.bmp
		}
		catch(e)
		{
			inform("Failed to load image.");
			throw e; // Displays a message, but rethrows the exception
		}
	}

	function test()
	{
		// Function that calls tryloadimage, returning true if image loading succeeds
		// and false otherwise
		try
		{
			tryloadimage();
		}
		catch
		{
			return false;
		}
		return true;
	}

　If test() is called here and image loading fails, a message will be displayed by the inform method, but because the exception is rethrown, it can be caught by the catch inside the test function.
